      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-5x+49}+4{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{-5x+49}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5x+49.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{-5x+49}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{-5x+49}}+12x^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{-5(0)+49}}+12(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
